# aldo: no debugger frames for functions invoked via apply/mapcar (object systems)

## Symptom
Under `--on-error debug`, an error signalled inside a function that was reached
through `apply` (or `mapcar`, `vl-catch-all-apply`, an indirect `eval`, …) breaks
into the debugger with an EMPTY backtrace: the stack shows only `<toplevel>`
(source `<repl>:1 (source unavailable)`), even though `(clal-optimization)` is
`(DEBUG 3)` and the code was loaded under the session.

Reported for an a-list object system whose `send` dispatches every method via
  (defun invoke-method (... function-name ...)
    (apply (eval function-name) (cons instance argument-list)))
so `(send col 'total-weight '())` erroring in the method's body shows no frames.
(~pjb/src/public/autocad-gui-prototype/object-system/)

## Root cause (confirmed empirically)
Debugger frames come only from a function's INSTRUMENTED body (the %CLAL-POLL
shadow stack, autolisp-debug/source/stepping.lisp debug-poll-enter). A function
invoked through the higher-order builtins runs its PLAIN (uninstrumented) body
under a debug session, so it fires no poll and pushes no frame.

Minimal repro (REPL, --on-error debug):
  (defun cc (x) (/ x 0))
  (defun bb (x) (apply 'cc (list x)))   ; via APPLY
  (defun aa (x) (bb x))
  (aa 5)
  => stop stack: BB <- AA <- <toplevel>   ; CC (the apply'd fn) is MISSING
Direct chain (bb calls (cc x)) instead shows CC <- BB <- AA <- <toplevel>.

A poll-level probe confirms: with `*debugging*` T and CC carrying an
instrumented body, CC's entry poll never fires when CC is reached via `apply`;
only AA and BB (direct calls) do. So the two-body instrumented-selection in
`call-autolisp-function-in-context` (autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp) is not
taking effect on the apply path — the callee runs plain.

## Fix direction
Ensure a usubr invoked through APPLY / MAPCAR / VL-EVERY / VL-*-IF / FOREACH-like
helpers / VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY / indirect EVAL runs its INSTRUMENTED body under a
debug session (two-body selection + lazy/eager weave), so it pushes a shadow
frame like a direct call. builtin-apply already funnels through
call-autolisp-function -> call-autolisp-function-in-context, which SELECTS the
instrumented body under *debugging* — yet empirically the plain body runs, so
the divergence between the direct-call path (api.lisp ~3515) and the
builtin-invocation path (call-autolisp-function, api.lisp 1859) must be found
and closed. User's suggestion: instrument lambda expressions dynamically when
they are passed to eval/apply, keyed on the optimization DEBUG level.

## Notes
- Depends on the DEBUG-level instrumentation wiring shipped in 1.9.65
  (clautolisp-version-bump); this ticket is the remaining, deeper cause.
- Hot evaluator code + large test corpus (runtime/builtins-core/debug) — land
  with the full suites green and add a regression that a function reached via
  apply produces >=1 frame at an error stop.

## RESOLVED (1.9.66)
Fixed by capturing the debugger's shadow call stack onto the
autolisp-runtime-error condition at SIGNAL time — the deepest, error point,
before any HANDLER-CASE unwinds it — and preferring it in BUILD-SNAPSHOT:

- autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp: *DEBUG-ERROR-SNAPSHOT-HOOK* + the condition's
  DEBUG-SNAPSHOT slot, whose :INITFORM calls the hook (an INITIALIZE-INSTANCE
  method on a condition is undefined and unrun on SBCL — the slot initform is the
  portable capture point, evaluated by MAKE-CONDITION at the error site).
- autolisp-debug/source/error.lisp: CAPTURE-ERROR-SHADOW-STACK installs the hook
  (copies the live shadow stack when a debug session is active; NIL otherwise, so
  ordinary runs pay nothing); DEBUG-HANDLE-ERROR passes the captured stack to
  BUILD-SNAPSHOT.
- autolisp-debug/source/snapshot.lisp: BUILD-SNAPSHOT takes an
  OVERRIDE-CALL-STACK, used over TI's live (now-unwound) stack.

This is immune to EVERY unwinding handler-case in the path (the per-subr
WRAP-BUILTIN-FUNCTION wrapper, the call-in-context subr branch, LOAD's *error*
handler), so a function reached via APPLY / MAPCAR / SEND-dispatch / LOAD shows a
full backtrace. Verified on the built tool: (apply 'cc …) → CC ← BB ← AA; the
reporter's object system loaded via (load) → LAMBDA ← INVOKE-METHOD ← SEND ←
<toplevel>. Tests: runtime capture-wiring + a debug-suite test that the snapshot
survives an unwinding handler-case between the error and the session. 3701
checks green across runtime/builtins/debug/dumb/ncurses/tool.
